        {
            "number": 4,
            "revision": "4e013fc737903f94e3070b31e71912c41bb9d70e",
            "parents": [
                "6bc465e971e9c1cacc569c209c5e704e5b9538ea"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/47/90547/4",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                "username": "jaehyoo"
            },
            "createdOn": 1780235445,
            "author": {
                "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                "username": "jaehyoo"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "doHttpUpdate flow is only targetted for BMC image updates. For any other update such as BIOS, please use Multipart form HTTP push.\n\nRefer to comment in BMCWeb -\n\n        // HTTP push only supports BMC updates (with ApplyTime as immediate) for\n        // backwards compatibility. Specific component updates will be handled\n        // through Multipart form HTTP push."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, I and Ravi also tried to fix it in the webui side but we met issues in there.\n@raviteja28031990@gmail.com Can you please share the details we met while you were fixing it in webui side?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "@jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com \n\nAs written in the description, you shouldn't be using doHTTPUpdate(HttpPushUri) path for BIOS update as that flow is only targeted for BMC image update (not BIOS).\n\nPlease test this flow directly via BMCWeb using Multipart form (MultipartHttpPushUri) based flow. If you find any issues via that flow, you can update this patch with the changes. In general, i am looking for a Tested section in this patch via Redfish MultipartHttpPushUri which shows the full set of logs.\n\nIf above works as expected, then you can debug from WebUI side and make any related changes there."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "@paligill@gmail.com\n\nAh, I need to fix the commit message.\n\nThe current WebUI uses MultipartHttpPushUri and always uploads to /redfish/v1/UpdateService/FirmwareInventory/bmc without checking the target purpose.  As a result, the following error occurs:\n\n```\nJun 17 02:45:08 qcom-bmc-ast2600-d6bc0bf5efe0 phosphor-software-manager[316]: Starting update for image 8\nJun 17 02:45:08 qcom-bmc-ast2600-d6bc0bf5efe0 phosphor-software-manager[316]: Creating Activation object for id: 7b2608ce\nJun 17 02:45:08 qcom-bmc-ast2600-d6bc0bf5efe0 phosphor-software-manager[316]: Extracting archive to: /tmp/images/imagewZj190\nJun 17 02:45:08 qcom-bmc-ast2600-d6bc0bf5efe0 mapperx[298]: Found invalid association on path /xyz/openbmc_project/software/7b2608ce\nJun 17 02:45:08 qcom-bmc-ast2600-d6bc0bf5efe0 mapperx[298]: Found invalid association on path /xyz/openbmc_project/software/7b2608ce\nJun 17 02:45:09 qcom-bmc-ast2600-d6bc0bf5efe0 phosphor-software-manager[316]: Purpose (xyz.openbmc_project.Software.Version.VersionPurpose.Host) is not supported\nJun 17 02:45:09 qcom-bmc-ast2600-d6bc0bf5efe0 phosphor-software-manager[316]: An error occurred processing the image.\nJun 17 02:45:09 qcom-bmc-ast2600-d6bc0bf5efe0 systemd[1]: systemd-journald.service: Sent signal SIGRTMIN+1 to main process 153 (systemd-journal) on client request.\n```\n\nFrom my testing, the issue does not occur when using Redfish multipart update directly instead of the WebUI. Uploading a BMC tarball to /redfish/v1/UpdateService/FirmwareInventory/bmc and a BIOS tarball to /redfish/v1/UpdateService/FirmwareInventory/bios_active works as expected.  This confirms that the issue is caused by the WebUI handling all tarballs the same way without distinguishing their purpose.\n\nI considered a few possible approaches to fix this.\n\nThe simplest solution would be to split the firmware update page into separate BMC and BIOS upload buttons so that the user can explicitly choose the target inventory path.  However, I thought that this breaks the current workflow where the tarball purpose is determined automatically. It would also require additional UI changes whenever new purposes (including 'Other') are introduced, so I ruled out this option.\n\nAnother idea was to have the WebUI frontend extract the tarball, parse the manifest file, determine the purpose, and then upload it to the appropriate inventory path.  I and Ravi experimented with this approach, but for compressed tarballs the frontend would first need to decompress the archive and inspect the manifest.  Supporting all archive formats in the frontend is not straightforward, and I am not convinced that this responsibility belongs in the frontend in the first place.\n\nFor these reasons, I concluded that the problem should be solved in the Software Manager layer, which is why I created this patch.\n\nThere may be a better or more correct approach.  I would appreciate hearing your thoughts if you have any suggestions."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "> The current WebUI uses MultipartHttpPushUri and always uploads to /redfish/v1/UpdateService/FirmwareInventory/bmc without checking the target purpose.\n\nIt's /redfish/v1/Managers/bmc actually."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "I think you answered your question - \n\n> to split the firmware update page into separate BMC and BIOS upload buttons so that the user can explicitly choose the target inventory path.\n\nI can understand the user can provide an un targeted bundle, but the user interface shall also be able to select the right target as thats one of the expected and implemented Redfish flows."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "> I can understand the user can provide an un targeted bundle, but the user interface shall also be able to select the right target as thats one of the expected and implemented Redfish flows.\n\nYeah. Ravi and I will try to add a dropdown in the WebUI to let users select the purpose. That way, we can reuse the existing file selector and upload button, making this a very minimal change. It would also eliminate the need to extract and inspect the manifest file from the compressed tarball just to determine the purpose."
                }
            ],
